---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/use-cases/holiday-gifting';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/holiday-gifting');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/holiday-gifting');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Holiday gifting</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">SMS marketing apps for holiday gifting</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A gift has a payer, a recipient, and a reveal date. Plan messages around those separate roles so a useful reminder does not expose a surprise, promise an arrival you cannot support, or turn a delivery address into a marketing audience.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Compare app workflows</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Coordinate email and SMS</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Separate identity, permission, and the surprise</h2>
      <p>Keep payer contact, intended recipient, delivery destination, and authorized notification destination as separate fields tied to the order. Do not merge people because they share a household, address, or phone. A purchaser entering someone else's number for delivery does not establish that person's promotional permission. Do not infer a recipient's preferences from the payer's purchases.</p>
      <p>Record which person supplied each contact, its intended use, the disclosure and permission evidence, and any requested reveal date and timezone. Ask the payer where confirmations and problems should go. Keep item names, prices, gift messages, and redeemable codes out of lock-screen previews and generic tracking texts where they could reveal the gift. Use authenticated support or a suitably protected order link for sensitive details; do not put gift secrets in link parameters.</p>
      <p>Separate necessary order service from optional gift offers. Classify each template by purpose, audience, and permission requirements before sending; adding a promotion to a delivery notice needs its own review. Apply the rules for the relevant destination, sender, channel, and purpose with qualified advice where needed. An operational label alone does not establish eligibility. See <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent planning</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours controls</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Use gift state to decide the next message</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Gift state</th><th>Useful action</th><th>Stop condition</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Physical gift under consideration</td><td>Show the selected variant, current availability, destination eligibility, and gift-wrap options on the landing page.</td><td>Pause if the size, bundle component, or delivery method is unavailable; a product-level stock flag is insufficient.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Physical gift ordered</td><td>Route fulfillment updates to the authorized contact; distinguish partial shipment, delay, and delivery.</td><td>Remove the purchased order from cart recovery and gift acquisition reminders.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Digital gift scheduled</td><td>Track paid, scheduled, issued, delivery attempted, failed, and redeemed as distinct events.</td><td>A successful payment is not proof that the recipient received or opened the gift.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Gift card redeemed in part</td><td>Provide a secure balance and help path when requested.</td><td>Do not send a new-buyer offer merely because a balance remains.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Gift returned or disputed</td><td>Route the case to support using the store's applicable gift-return process.</td><td>Pause review requests, replenishment, and celebratory gifting messages while unresolved.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Carry order and line-item identifiers through these states. One payer may buy gifts for several people and a self-purchase in the same checkout. Preserve the distinction so one delivered parcel does not mark every gift delivered or enroll the household in a replenishment journey.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Shipping estimates and cutoff zones</h2>
      <p>Build a cutoff record for each eligible destination zone, fulfillment location, service, and product class. Include processing time, customization or preorder lead time, warehouse closure dates, carrier pickup assumptions, and the time and timezone of the last update. Check the actual variant and destination again at checkout. Stock and a shipping label do not prove carrier acceptance or holiday arrival.</p>
      <p>Describe a cutoff as an ordering estimate with its conditions, not an arrival guarantee. Show a dated estimate and the relevant zone on the linked page. A sender's timezone controls neither the buyer's local clock nor the warehouse cutoff. If destination or timing is unknown, link to an estimate lookup rather than inventing a deadline. Suppress physical-gift urgency after the applicable cutoff; offer digital gifts only where their issuance and delivery path is working.</p>
      <p>Define how often estimates must be refreshed based on operational volatility. If the feed is stale or the carrier changes service expectations, pause queued deadline messages, update the page, and have an owner approve a correction for affected buyers. Do not replay expired urgency after a feed recovers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Gift cards, returns, and gift subscriptions</h2>
      <p>Shopify documents that a gift-card balance can span orders and apply to taxes and shipping. It also distinguishes the first payment on a subscription order from recurring subscription payments, which gift cards cannot cover. Use the <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/gift-card-products/redeem-gift-card">official redemption guidance</a> alongside your actual checkout configuration before describing how a gift card works.</p>
      <p>Explain currency and checkout eligibility, how the recipient accesses the card, and where to get balance or redemption help. Keep issuance, delivery, and redemption separate in reporting. Treat the code as value-bearing information: avoid copying it into promotional texts, shared dashboards, or analytics events. A resend request needs identity checks and a single owner so two systems do not create duplicate delivery attempts.</p>
      <p>Publish the applicable gift-return window and process, proof-of-purchase options, refund destination, exchange or store-credit choices, and any exceptions. Distinguish returning merchandise bought with a card from refunding the purchase of the card itself. Do not promise a cash refund or tell the recipient the payer's financial details without checking the order, policy, and authority.</p>
      <p>For subscriptions, record who pays, who receives, the prepaid term, renewal behavior, and who can change address, skip, cancel, or authorize additional charges. Do not convert a fixed-term gift into recipient billing by default. Route payment failures to the authorized payer and delivery problems to the appropriate contact without exposing billing data. Before an expiry or renewal reminder, recheck the contract and permissions; do not treat a recipient's gift activation as promotional opt-in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>One decision across gifting and other journeys</h2>
      <p>Give each queued message a purpose, person, order or gift identifier, validity window, and deduplication key. Recheck eligibility at dispatch against opt-outs, purpose-specific permission, purchase state, open support issues, gift privacy settings, experiment assignments, and the shared contact calendar. Preserve the scope of each opt-out instead of assuming an SMS change authorizes an email send or cancels every necessary service message.</p>
      <p>A completed gift order should exit its cart and browse reminders across tools. A recipient should not enter the payer's loyalty, winback, review, or replenishment flows. A self-purchase line can have its own lifecycle only if it can be identified reliably. Establish message priority and contact limits for your audience; do not let a gift deadline text collide with a welcome discount, Black Friday offer, or subscription renewal reminder. Relevant journeys include <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery</a>, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase</a>, and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">replenishment</a>.</p>
      <p>When events are delayed or identity is ambiguous, hold the promotion and assign an operator to resolve it. Retry only after checking the latest order state, permission, and deadline; preserve idempotency across retries and provider changes. For a mistaken reveal or wrong recipient, stop further messages, restrict exposed links where possible, route to support, and agree a correction with the authorized contact rather than sending a second revealing blast.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Choose software against the gift workflow</h2>
      <p>A unified email and SMS workflow is a useful fit when the team can maintain one identity model and prove that purchase exits and recipient restrictions work across both channels. Separate senders can fit when an owner reconciles events and suppression between them. An email-led workflow with optional SMS can fit when gift instructions need detail and texts serve a narrow, permitted purpose.</p>
      <p>Use <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a>, and <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> as evaluation starting points. Ask each shortlisted supplier to demonstrate your gift states, identity separation, permission mapping, scheduled-send cancellation, access restrictions, event freshness, and export/recovery procedure in your configuration. Confirm supported markets and current contract costs directly. These are selection requirements, not claims that every app implements them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical scenarios and proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These examples are proposed checks for a store's workflow, not merchant results or promises of app behavior.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Shared household:</strong> a payer orders a surprise for a recipient at the same address. Expect separate identities, confirmation only to the authorized contact, and no recipient marketing enrollment.</li>
        <li><strong>Variant sold out:</strong> the red scarf sells out while blue remains available. Expect the red-scarf reminder to stop or link to an honest availability choice, with no automatic substitution.</li>
        <li><strong>Two cutoff zones:</strong> a warehouse cutoff has passed for one destination but remains open for another. Expect zone-specific eligibility and dated estimates; unknown destinations receive no arrival claim.</li>
        <li><strong>Scheduled digital gift:</strong> payment succeeds but delivery fails before the requested reveal. Expect a controlled support task for the authorized payer, no delivered state, and no blind duplicate resend.</li>
        <li><strong>Partial redemption and return:</strong> a recipient spends part of a gift card and returns an item. Expect balance and refund handling reconciled with the order and policy, without marketing enrollment or payer-detail disclosure.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed-term subscription:</strong> the last prepaid box ships. Expect an expiry notice only to an eligible contact, with no unapproved charge or automatic recipient renewal.</li>
        <li><strong>Late purchase event:</strong> checkout completes while cart and holiday reminders are queued in different tools. Expect both to recheck the order and suppress duplicates before dispatch.</li>
        <li><strong>Complaint after a reveal:</strong> the gift recipient reports an unwanted message. Expect promotional queues held, permission and contact mapping investigated, and scoped suppression preserved through recovery.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Measure contribution without counting the gift twice</h2>
      <p>Define the eligible payer audience and randomize a no-promotion holdout before sending, keeping assignments consistent across channels and repeat orders. Continue necessary service communications in both groups. Measure over a window that includes the holiday and meaningful returns or redemption lag. Report uncertainty and sample size; attributed revenue alone cannot establish incremental lift.</p>
      <p>As a hypothetical arithmetic example, contribution after merchandise cost, discounts, fulfillment, payment fees, and returns is $1,200 for 1,000 eligible payers in the send group and $1,000 for 1,000 holdout payers. The difference is $0.20 per eligible payer, or $200 across the send group. If incremental messaging and operating costs total $150, the estimated net difference is $50. This is an illustrative estimate, not a proven effect; avoid subtracting a cost twice.</p>
      <p>Track card issuance, cash collection, redemption, outstanding balance, and merchandise contribution separately. Do not add gift-card sale value and the full redemption order value as two independent revenue wins; reconcile recognition with your accounting owner. Split physical gifts, digital gifts, and subscriptions, and report delivery failures, late-arrival contacts, accidental reveals, opt-outs, refunds, and support cost beside contribution. Check the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> against peak-season volume before expanding the program.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
